Carafe: with d3d9.managedBufferCopies on top of d3d9.frontEndThread, a lock of a managed buffer no longer waits for
the queue. The thread that queues keeps a copy of the buffer, made from the buffer's own memory the second time it is
locked, so buffers filled once while loading are not copied: a lock hands out that copy, a read-only lock costs
nothing more, and the unlock queues the changed range, which the worker writes into the buffer in order with the
draws. Any lock of the buffer outside the queue, from another thread or one too large to queue, makes the copy be
taken again. The effect worker hands such uploads on to the DXVK worker instead of locking the buffer itself. With the
profiler on, the copies made are counted in the front end line.
Zlib, as the files it changes.

